    {
      "type": "stage",
      "kicker": "The scale",
      "narration": "How often do people survive? Outside hospital, about 350,000 people a year in the United States have a cardiac arrest, and about 90% of them die. Survival to hospital discharge is about 10.5%: 21.1% when the arrest happens in a public place, but only 8.9% at home. In hospital, survival is higher: about 23.6% for adults, and 45.2% for children. Yet only about 41% of people receive compressions from a bystander before emergency services arrive.",
