    {
      "type": "stage",
      "kicker": "The first minutes",
      "narration": "This is why the first minutes matter. Cardiac arrest is the only emergency in which the actions of the nearest person in the first three minutes matter more than everything done by everyone else afterwards. The arrested heart moves no blood at all. Each chest compression squeezes it, and pushes blood out into the aorta and the pulmonary trunk. Started immediately, compressions can double or even triple the chance of survival.",
